---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import FAQSection from '@components/article/FAQSection.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro';

// Article metadata
const title = 'Essential Travel Tech for Digital Nomads (2025)';
const description = 'The must-have gadgets and accessories for working from anywhere. Compact, reliable tech that fits in a carry-on and keeps you productive on the road.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2025-11-23');
const readingTime = 10;
const image = 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1519389950473-47ba0277781c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80';
const imageAlt = 'Digital nomad travel tech setup with laptop and accessories';

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  {text: 'Introduction', slug: 'introduction', depth: 2},
  {text: 'The Essentials', slug: 'essentials', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Connectivity', slug: 'connectivity', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Productivity Boosters', slug: 'productivity', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Power Solutions', slug: 'power', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Travel Comfort', slug: 'comfort', depth: 2},
  {text: 'FAQ', slug: 'faq', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Conclusion', slug: 'conclusion', depth: 2},
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Do I need a separate mobile hotspot or is phone tethering enough?',
    answer: '<p>Phone tethering works for backup, but dedicated hotspots like Skyroam or local SIM-based hotspots offer better battery management, can connect multiple devices, and don\'t drain your phone. If you work from cafes with unreliable WiFi, a hotspot is worth it.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What\'s the minimum laptop spec for remote work?',
    answer: '<p>For most remote work (writing, coding, video calls): 8GB RAM minimum (16GB preferred), SSD storage, and a recent processor. Battery life matters more than raw power—aim for 8+ hours. MacBook Air M2/M3, ThinkPad X1 Carbon, and Dell XPS 13 are popular choices.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I bring a monitor for traveling?',
    answer: '<p>Portable monitors (15-17") add significant productivity but also weight and bulk. Worth it for extended stays (1+ months) or if your work requires screen real estate. Skip them for fast-paced travel. The ASUS ZenScreen and Espresso Display are good lightweight options.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I keep my gear safe while traveling?',
    answer: '<p>Never check electronics in luggage. Use a TSA-approved laptop sleeve. Consider a cable lock for cafes (though it won\'t stop determined theft). Get electronics coverage on your travel insurance. Back up to cloud constantly. The best security is never leaving gear unattended.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What about international adapters?',
    answer: '<p>A universal adapter covers 99% of situations. Make sure it has USB-C and USB-A ports. GaN chargers with multiple ports can replace multiple adapters. Check your laptop charger—many support 100-240V automatically and just need a plug adapter, not a voltage converter.</p>'
  }
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Best Laptops for Remote Work',
    excerpt: 'Find the perfect laptop for productivity on the go.',
    href: '/tools/best-laptops-remote-work',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1496181133206-80ce9b88a853?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Laptop on desk'
  },
  {
    title: 'Top Destinations for Digital Nomads',
    excerpt: 'Cities with great WiFi, affordable living, and thriving communities.',
    href: '/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1528181304800-259b08848526?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Digital nomad destination'
  },
  {
    title: 'How to Find Reliable WiFi Anywhere',
    excerpt: 'Tips and tools for staying connected no matter where you travel.',
    href: '/blog/find-reliable-wifi-anywhere',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1521791136064-7986c2920216?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'WiFi signal concept'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  author={author}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  pubDate={pubDate}
  readingTime={readingTime}
  image={image}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  category="Travel"
  categorySlug="guides/travel"
  headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Packing Light, Working Heavy</h2>
  <p>
    The digital nomad dream comes with a practical challenge: you need to be productive from
    anywhere while keeping your bag light enough to actually travel. Every gram matters when
    you're hopping flights and navigating foreign transit.
  </p>
  <p>
    After years of testing gear across dozens of countries, we've distilled the essential
    tech setup for working remotely. This isn't about having the fanciest gadgets—it's about
    reliable, compact equipment that lets you work from a beach bar or a business center
    equally well.
  </p>

  <!-- The Essentials -->
  <h2 id="essentials">The Essentials (Don't Leave Home Without)</h2>

  <h3>Laptop</h3>
  <p>
    Your laptop is your office. Prioritize: battery life (8+ hours), weight (under 1.5kg/3.3lbs),
    reliability, and a good keyboard.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best overall:</strong> MacBook Air M3 - All-day battery, excellent performance, silent</li>
    <li><strong>Best Windows:</strong> ThinkPad X1 Carbon - Business-grade build, great keyboard</li>
    <li><strong>Budget pick:</strong> ThinkPad T14s - Excellent value, repairable</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/tools/best-laptops-remote-work">full laptop comparison</a> for detailed reviews.
  </p>

  <h3>Noise-Canceling Headphones</h3>
  <p>
    Non-negotiable for focus in noisy environments. ANC headphones transform a loud cafe into
    a quiet office.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Over-ear:</strong> Sony WH-1000XM5 or Bose QuietComfort Ultra - Best noise canceling</li>
    <li><strong>Compact:</strong> AirPods Pro 2 or Sony WF-1000XM5 - Pack smaller, still excellent</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Over-ear sounds better; earbuds pack easier. Most nomads end up with both eventually.
  </p>

  <h3>External Mouse (Optional but Recommended)</h3>
  <p>
    A good mouse reduces wrist strain and improves precision. Compact travel mice strike the
    balance between portability and ergonomics.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compact:</strong> Logitech MX Anywhere 3 - Best travel mouse</li>
    <li><strong>Budget:</strong> Logitech Pebble - Cheap, silent, works great</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Connectivity -->
  <h2 id="connectivity">Connectivity (Your Lifeline)</h2>

  <h3>Mobile Hotspot</h3>
  <p>
    Never rely solely on cafe WiFi. A mobile hotspot ensures you can always get online, even
    when local internet fails.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>International roaming:</strong> Solis or Skyroam - Works in 100+ countries</li>
    <li><strong>Local SIM:</strong> Get a local SIM with data, use phone as hotspot</li>
    <li><strong>Best value:</strong> Buy a cheap unlocked hotspot device, swap local SIMs</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>eSIM</h3>
  <p>
    eSIM has revolutionized nomad connectivity. No more hunting for SIM cards at airports—download
    data plans instantly.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best apps:</strong> Airalo, Holafly, Nomad eSIM</li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Compare prices; they vary wildly by country</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>VPN Service</h3>
  <p>
    Essential for security on public WiFi and accessing region-locked content. Install before
    you leave home.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Top picks:</strong> NordVPN, ExpressVPN, or Surfshark</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> Security, privacy, and accessing home streaming services</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">VPN comparison</a> for full reviews.
  </p>

  <h3>USB-C Hub</h3>
  <p>
    Modern laptops skimp on ports. A compact hub adds USB-A, HDMI, and SD card slots when needed.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minimal:</strong> Anker 5-in-1 USB-C Hub</li>
    <li><strong>Full-featured:</strong> CalDigit USB-C SOHO Dock</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Productivity Boosters -->
  <h2 id="productivity">Productivity Boosters</h2>

  <h3>Portable Monitor</h3>
  <p>
    A second screen significantly boosts productivity, especially for developers, designers,
    and anyone doing research-heavy work.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lightweight:</strong> ASUS ZenScreen MB16AC (0.8kg)</li>
    <li><strong>Premium:</strong> Espresso Display - Touchscreen, portrait mode</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Verdict:</strong> Worth it for stays of 1+ month. Skip for fast travel.
  </p>

  <h3>Laptop Stand</h3>
  <p>
    Elevating your screen to eye level prevents neck strain. Crucial for long work sessions.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compact:</strong> Roost Laptop Stand - Folds flat, very light</li>
    <li><strong>Budget:</strong> Nexstand K2 - Heavier but much cheaper</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>External Keyboard (Optional)</h3>
  <p>
    If using a laptop stand, you'll need an external keyboard. Choose compact/wireless.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compact:</strong> Logitech MX Keys Mini or Apple Magic Keyboard</li>
    <li><strong>Folding:</strong> iClever BK08 - Folds to pocket size</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Webcam Light</h3>
  <p>
    Video call lighting in hotels and cafes is often terrible. A small ring light improves
    your on-camera presence dramatically.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compact:</strong> Lume Cube Video Conference Lighting Kit</li>
    <li><strong>Budget:</strong> Any clip-on ring light from Amazon</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Power Solutions -->
  <h2 id="power">Power Solutions</h2>

  <h3>GaN Charger</h3>
  <p>
    Gallium Nitride chargers are smaller and lighter than traditional chargers at the same
    wattage. One multi-port charger can replace 3-4 separate adapters.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best value:</strong> Anker 735 (65W) - Laptop + phone + tablet from one charger</li>
    <li><strong>Compact:</strong> Anker Nano II 65W - Smallest 65W charger</li>
    <li><strong>High power:</strong> Anker 747 (150W) - For power-hungry setups</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Universal Travel Adapter</h3>
  <p>
    One adapter that works everywhere. Make sure it includes USB ports.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recommended:</strong> Zendure Passport III - USB-C PD, compact</li>
    <li><strong>Budget:</strong> EPICKA Universal Adapter - Covers most needs</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Power Bank</h3>
  <p>
    For long flights, bus rides, or cafes without accessible outlets. USB-C PD for fast
    laptop charging.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flight-friendly:</strong> Anker 737 (24,000mAh) - Max airline capacity</li>
    <li><strong>Compact:</strong> Anker 621 (5,000mAh) - Phone top-ups only</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Note:</strong> Airlines limit batteries to 100Wh for carry-on. Check capacity before flying.
  </p>

  <!-- Travel Comfort -->
  <h2 id="comfort">Travel Comfort</h2>

  <h3>Tech Organizer</h3>
  <p>
    Keep cables, adapters, and small gadgets organized. Saves time and prevents tangles.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minimal:</strong> Peak Design Tech Pouch</li>
    <li><strong>Budget:</strong> BAGSMART Electronics Organizer</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cable Kit</h3>
  <p>
    Bring the cables you need, no more:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>2x USB-C to USB-C (one long, one short)</li>
    <li>1x USB-C to Lightning (if using Apple devices)</li>
    <li>1x USB-A to USB-C (for older ports)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Backup Storage</h3>
  <p>
    Cloud storage is primary, but having local backup protects against connectivity issues.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compact SSD:</strong> Samsung T7 (500GB-2TB) - Fast, rugged, pocket-sized</li>
    <li><strong>Budget:</strong> SanDisk Extreme Portable SSD</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs} />

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">The Perfect Pack</h2>
  <p>
    Here's our recommended kit for most digital nomads:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Core:</strong> Laptop, noise-canceling headphones, mouse, GaN charger</li>
    <li><strong>Connectivity:</strong> eSIM, VPN subscription, USB-C hub</li>
    <li><strong>Comfort:</strong> Laptop stand, tech organizer, power bank</li>
    <li><strong>Optional:</strong> Portable monitor, external keyboard, webcam light</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Start minimal. You can always buy gear on the road (often cheaper), and you'll quickly
    learn what you actually need versus what seemed important at home.
  </p>
  <p>
    The best tech setup is one you'll actually carry and use. Prioritize reliability over
    features, and remember: you're traveling to work and live, not to have the perfect desk setup.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planning your first nomad trip? Check our <a href="/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads">
    destination guide</a> and make sure you have <a href="/blog/best-travel-insurance-remote-workers">
    proper travel insurance</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles} />
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
